genet.device - Broadcom GENETv5 Ethernet
========================================

The gigabit Ethernet controller on the BCM2711, which is the SoC a
Raspberry Pi 4 is built around. One controller, one unit, no options.


Where the code comes from
-------------------------

The register map and the hardware bring-up sequences are ported from
FreeBSD's driver for the same part:

    sys/arm64/broadcom/genet/if_genet.c
    sys/arm64/broadcom/genet/if_genetreg.h
        Copyright (c) 2020 Michael J Karels
        Copyright (c) 2016, 2020 Jared McNeill
        Two-clause BSD licence

which derive in turn from NetBSD's bcmgenet.c, also by Jared McNeill and
also two-clause BSD. That licence permits reuse in source form provided
the copyright notice, the conditions and the disclaimer travel with the
code, and they do: the full notice is at the top of genet.h, and the
files carrying ported sequences point at it.

That is a deliberate choice of source. The previous driver in this
directory was adapted from U-Boot's drivers/net/bcmgenet.c, which is
GPL-2.0+, while carrying an "All rights reserved" AROS header and no
LEGAL file - the licence stated and the licence owed did not match.
Rather than correct the paperwork, the derivation was moved to a
permissively licensed source. No part of this driver comes from U-Boot's
bcmgenet or from Linux's, and neither was consulted while writing it.

The SANA-II side - the opener list, the deferred request queues, the unit
task, the command set - is AROS code, written to the same shape as
workbench/devs/networks/dwmac.


What the hardware does differently
----------------------------------

Three things about this controller are unusual enough that code written
on ordinary assumptions will not work, and all three cost the previous
driver dearly.

*The DMA descriptors are registers, not memory.* They live inside the
register block, 256 per direction, twelve bytes each. There is no
descriptor ring in RAM, so there is nothing about the descriptors to keep
coherent and no physical address to compute for them. Only the packet
buffers they point at are in RAM.

*The queue is number 16.* The block has seventeen queues per direction;
sixteen priority ones and a seventeenth, index 16, where unclassified
traffic arrives. Setting up queue 0 looks entirely reasonable and
receives nothing at all.

*Nothing is cache coherent.* The controller reaches memory through the
SoC's system bus, and its accesses are not snooped by the processor
caches. Packet buffers have to be memory the processor does not cache,
which this driver arranges with KrnMapGlobal() and MAP_WriteThrough.
Buffers left cacheable produce a driver that initialises cleanly, reports
a link, and moves no data.

MAP_WriteThrough is worth a word, because the name and the effect are not
quite the same thing. On this platform the kernel maps that flag to
Normal Non-Cacheable, which is what these buffers need and is stated as
such in arch/aarch64-native/kernel/mmu.c. A port whose kernel reads the
flag as write-back-with-write-through would give this driver cacheable
buffers and the symptom above, so the mapping is worth checking before
this code is trusted on other aarch64 hardware.

The register block needs the matching treatment in the other direction:
it is mapped MAP_CacheInhibit, so accesses to it are device accesses.
Mapped as ordinary memory - which is what KrnMapGlobal() does when told
neither MAP_CacheInhibit nor MAP_WriteThrough - register writes would sit
in a cache line instead of reaching the block.


Interrupt numbering
-------------------

The device tree states interrupts as a GIC specifier: a kind, a number
within that kind, and flags. AROS counts in raw GIC interrupt ids, and so
does the GIC itself, so the base of the kind has to be added back on -
shared peripheral interrupts start at 32, private ones at 16. The Pi 4's
tree gives the controller SPI 157 and 158, which are interrupt ids 189
and 190.

The previous driver used 157 directly. That is a valid interrupt id; it
belongs to something else.

Only the first of the two is taken. The second is raised for PHY and link
events, which this driver learns about by asking the PHY once a second
from the unit task instead.


What is deliberately not implemented
------------------------------------

*Checksum offload*, in either direction. Enabling it puts a 64-byte
status block on the front of every packet, and the receive path reads its
lengths straight out of the descriptor instead.

*Duplex.* This register map has no duplex bit, and the reference driver
writes none. A gigabit link is full duplex in any case; a half duplex
10/100 link is left to the PHY's own collision handling. The negotiated
duplex is recorded and reported, and otherwise unused.

*Priority queues.* One queue per direction is set up and given the whole
descriptor area.

*Multicast ranges wider than one address.* The hardware filter matches
whole addresses, seventeen of them, while SANA-II hands out ranges. A
range covering one address fits a filter slot. A wider one does not, and
no filter setting expresses it, so it puts the receiver in promiscuous
mode and the sorting is done in software by GENET_AddressFilter(), which
compares against the ranges properly.


Files
-----

genet.h            register map, private structures, the BSD notice
genet_dt.c         finding the controller in the device tree
genet_hw.c         registers, mdio, PHY, reset
genet_unit.c       rings, interrupt, buffers, the unit task
genet_handler.c    the SANA-II command set
genet_init.c       device init, open and close


A note on CacheClearE
---------------------

genet_unit.c does its own cache maintenance around the one point where a
mapping changes between cacheable and not. That work belongs in
CacheClearE(), and calling it is what the code ought to do - but on
aarch64 CacheClearE() is currently a stub that returns without doing
anything. When exec grows a real implementation, genet_cacheflush()
should give way to it.
